tps://www.ssdnodes.com/learn/tailscale-serve-command-cheat-sheet"}]}, {"@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Does tailscale serve survive a reboot?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Only with --bg. A background handler is written into the node's persistent serve config and comes back after a reboot, and after tailscale down followed by tailscale up. A foreground handler is stored under the CLI's watch session and is removed the moment that session ends, whether by Ctrl+C or by a closed SSH window."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can I use tailscale serve without enabling HTTPS certificates?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes, in two modes. --http=\u003cport> serves plain HTTP inside the tailnet, and --tcp=\u003cport> forwards raw TCP. Neither asks for a certificate. Only the default HTTPS mode needs MagicDNS and HTTPS certificates enabled on the DNS page of the admin console, and if they are not, the CLI prints an approval URL and waits rather than failing."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Why does my app return 404 for every path under --set-path?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Serve strips the mount path before forwarding. A request for /api/users reaches the backend as /users. An app configured to live under /api never sees its prefix and answers 404. Fix it by putting the prefix on the target as well, --set-path=/api http://127.0.0.1:8080/api, so the proxy joins it back on after stripping."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How do I remove a single serve handler without clearing the rest?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Repeat the flags that created it and append off, for example sudo tailscale serve --https=443 --set-path=/api off. Run sudo tailscale serve status first to see the exact port and path. sudo tailscale serve reset clears every handler on the node instead, including Funnel."}}]}]}</script>
  
  
